<task id="021" project="email-communication-solution-r4">
  <metadata>
    <title>Build the three thin semantic wrappers — `&lt;SendEmailStep /&gt;` / `&lt;SendEmailDialog /&gt;` / `&lt;SendEmailPage /&gt;`</title>
    <phase>Wave 2 — Client: composer engine + wrappers</phase>
    <status>not-started</status>
    <estimated-hours>5</estimated-hours>
    <dependencies>020</dependencies>
    <blocks>060, 061</blocks>
    <tags>frontend, fluent-ui, react, communication</tags>
    <rigor-hint>FULL</rigor-hint>
    <rigor-reason>These wrappers are the caller-facing API for every email-send surface in Spaarke (wizards, dialogs, Code Page); they modify `.tsx` in the shared library and set the contract that W6 caller migrations (060/061) and the W4 Code Page (041) consume. Each is thin, but the prop-surface + `mount`-locking decisions are contract-level; a wrong prop shape ripples across every caller. FULL.</rigor-reason>
    <parallel-group>W2</parallel-group>
    <parallel-safe>true</parallel-safe>
    <parallel-reason>All-client TypeScript disjoint from the W1 server track. Depends on 020's engine files; runs safely alongside W1/W3/W7. The three wrappers are independent of each other but small enough to ship in one task.</parallel-reason>
    <fr-refs>FR-12</fr-refs>
    <model-tier>sonnet</model-tier>
    <effort>high</effort>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>
    Build the three canonical semantic wrappers in `src/client/shared/Spaarke.UI.Components/src/components/EmailComposer/wrappers/`. Each is ~30–60 LOC, locks one `mount` value, and exposes a tighter semantic prop API for its context. Wrappers contain NO business logic — they delegate to `&lt;EmailComposer /&gt;` (task 020). Callers import a wrapper, NOT the engine directly (design §5.1.2 — direct engine use requires a code-review-justified comment).

    Authoring detail: `reference/r3-send-side-design.md` §5.1.1 (wrapper prop interfaces + delegation), §5.1.2 (when to use engine vs wrapper), §5.3 (mount shapes). Reuse the exact prop shapes there.

    1. `SendEmailStep.tsx` — wizard-step caller. Locks `mount='inline'`, default `mode='compose'`. `forwardRef` passes `composerRef` through so the wizard host drives `validate()`/`send()` on its own Finish button. NO internal action bar (engine's ComposerActionBar returns null on inline). Props: `composerRef`, `mode?`, `initialTo?`, `initialSubject?`, `initialBody?`, `initialBodyFormat?`, `associations?`, `attachmentSources?`, `wizardContext?`, `authenticatedFetch`, `bffBaseUrl`, `onStateChange?`.

    2. `SendEmailDialog.tsx` — popup caller (Fluent Dialog). Locks `mount='dialog'`, default `mode='compose'`. Owns open/close: `open`, `onClose`; wires engine `onSent` → `props.onSent?.(communicationId)` then `onClose()`. Props: `open`, `onClose`, `mode?`, `initialTo?`, `initialSubject?`, `associations?`, `attachmentSources?`, `authenticatedFetch`, `bffBaseUrl`, `onSent?`, `onError?`. (This replaces the old single-caller `SendEmailDialog` — it becomes the canonical dialog wrapper; W6 FilePreviewDialog migration consumes it.)

    3. `SendEmailPage.tsx` — Code Page caller. Locks `mount='page'`, `mode` is REQUIRED (driven by the Code Page URL parameter). Props: `mode`, `communicationId?`, `initialTo?`, `initialSubject?`, `initialBody?`, `associations?`, `authenticatedFetch`, `bffBaseUrl`, `onSent?`, `onClose?` (mapped to engine `onCancel`). (W4 task 041 mounts this into the Communication Code Page.)

    Export all three wrappers + their prop interfaces from `EmailComposer/index.ts` and confirm they surface through the shared-lib `src/index.ts` barrel.
  </prompt>

  <role>
    Frontend developer comfortable with the thin-wrapper + `forwardRef` pass-through pattern and Fluent v9 Dialog composition.
  </role>

  <goal>
    Three wrappers compile lint-clean, each ≤~60 LOC, each locking its `mount` value with the engine prop hard-coded. `SendEmailStep` forwards `composerRef`; `SendEmailDialog` owns open/close and maps `onSent`; `SendEmailPage` requires `mode` and maps `onClose`→`onCancel`. All three + prop types exported through the barrels. No `@spaarke/auth` import (injected `authenticatedFetch` only). `npm run build:prod` + `npm run lint` clean.
  </goal>

  <context>
    <background>
      Per FR-12 + design §5.1.1: thin wrappers carry the semantic prop API; the engine (020) carries all logic. The default code-review expectation is that callers import a wrapper. W6 consumers: `SendEmailStep` ← the shared `CreateRecordWizard/steps/SendEmailStep.tsx` (Project/Event/Todo/WorkAssignment) + `CreateMatter` fork (task 061); `SendEmailDialog` ← FilePreviewDialog + SummarizeFilesDialog (task 060); `SendEmailPage` ← the Communication Code Page (task 041).

      Empirical Finding #5 (R4): only `CreateMatter/SendEmailStep.tsx` is a true LegalWorkspace fork — the other four wizards share the canonical `CreateRecordWizard/steps/SendEmailStep.tsx`. This wrapper is what that shared file will render after migration.
    </background>
    <relevant-files>
      <file>src/client/shared/Spaarke.UI.Components/src/components/EmailComposer/wrappers/SendEmailStep.tsx (NEW)</file>
      <file>src/client/shared/Spaarke.UI.Components/src/components/EmailComposer/wrappers/SendEmailDialog.tsx (NEW)</file>
      <file>src/client/shared/Spaarke.UI.Components/src/components/EmailComposer/wrappers/SendEmailPage.tsx (NEW)</file>
